  "query_preview": "/Through Thursday/...\nIssued at 302 PM MDT Thu Aug 27 2026\n\nThe upper level high and ridge axis shifts over Colorado today, \nbringing warmer temperatures and drier air. Precipitable water \nvalues fall to near normal (0.6-0.9\") across the area. MLCAPE \nwill be around 500 j/kg, so we should see some shower/thunderstorm\nactivity. However, mid level warming is expected to limit development\nthis afternoon and evening. With DCAPE values of 1000-1500 j/kg, \ngusty outflow winds will be the main issue with gusty up to 50 \nmph. Can't rule out a few isolated gusts near 60 mph. Brief moderate\nto heavy rain will also accompany the thunderstorms. The showers/storms\nend by mid evening as the airmass stabilizes. Thunderstorms tracking\nsouth over western Nebraska could clip the far northeast corner \nof Colorado later this evening/tonight. These storms will be in a \nmore moist and unstable airmass, with MLCAPE of 1000-2000 j/kg and\nbe severe if the pass over the area. \n\nThe upper level high that has been to our southwest will begin\nshifting eastward Friday as an upper level trough moves onto the \nPacific Northwest coast. Temperatures continue to warm with highs \nexpected to reach the lower to mid 90s across northeast Colorado. \nSome models show a subtle increase in moisture, others dont. If we\nsee this increase, it would just increase the number of high- \nbased showers and thunderstorms. Gusty outflow winds up to 50 mph \nwill again be the main threat. Southwest flow aloft will increase \nSaturday throughout the day as the upper-level trough moves inland\nand the upper-level high shifts southeast of the Central Rockies.\nWe will see one more day of very warm temperatures with highs in \nthe lower to mid 90s over northeast Colorado. Moisture increases, \nmainly late in the day. This will lead to a better chance for \nshowers and thunderstorms.\n\nOn Sunday, southwest flow aloft will increase becoming somewhat \nstrong for this time of year as the upper level trough moves \nacross Montana. This will increase monsoon (mid/upper-level) \nmoisture. However, with a lack of a cold front or easterly push \nlow-level moisture will be limited. We still expect a round of \nscattered afternoon/evening showers and thunderstorms. Temperatures\nbegin to cool, but remain slightly above normal. Much of the same\nis expected for Monday, except that a shortwave trough embedded \nin the southwest flow could bring a better chance for showers if \nit tracks over the region. \n\nFor Tuesday through Thursday, southwest flow aloft will continue \nwith the upper level high remaining southeast of the region and \ntroughiness to our north. As for shower and thunderstorm chances, \nthey decrease as the airmass dries. There may be one or two days \nwith no convection, but it is too soon to tell when.",
